WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.238 --> 00:00:08.392
In order to create
an application where you

2
00:00:08.392 --> 00:00:11.084
can chat with your data,
you first have to load

3
00:00:11.084 --> 00:00:14.314
your data into a format
where it can be worked with. That's

4
00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:16.468
where LangChain document loaders
come into play. We

5
00:00:16.468 --> 00:00:18.891
have over 80 different
types of document loaders, and

6
00:00:18.891 --> 00:00:21.314
in this lesson we'll cover
a few of the

7
00:00:21.314 --> 00:00:23.467
most important ones and
get you comfortable with

8
00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:24.544
the concept in general.


9
00:00:25.145 --> 00:00:25.666
Let's jump in!

10
00:00:28.241 --> 00:00:31.370
Document loaders deal
with the specifics of accessing and

11
00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:34.499
converting data from a variety of
different formats and

12
00:00:34.499 --> 00:00:37.628
sources into a standardized format.
There can be different

13
00:00:37.628 --> 00:00:41.105
places that we want
to load data from, like websites,

14
00:00:41.105 --> 00:00:43.539
different databases, YouTube,
and these documents can

15
00:00:43.539 --> 00:00:46.668
come in different data types,
like PDFs, HTML, JSON.

16
00:00:46.668 --> 00:00:49.449
And so the whole
purpose of document loaders

17
00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:52.230
is to take this
variety of data sources

18
00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:55.012
and load them into
a standard document object.

19
00:00:55.012 --> 00:00:57.793
Which consists of content
and then associated metadata.

20
00:00:57.793 --> 00:01:00.574
There are a lot
of different type of

21
00:01:00.574 --> 00:01:03.008
document loaders in LangChain,
and we won't

22
00:01:03.008 --> 00:01:06.833
have time to cover them all, but
here is a rough

23
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:09.962
categorization of the 80
plus that we have. There

24
00:01:09.962 --> 00:01:13.438
are a lot that deal
with loading unstructured data, like

25
00:01:13.438 --> 00:01:15.872
text files, from
public data sources, like

26
00:01:15.872 --> 00:01:19.696
YouTube, Twitter, Hacker News, and
there are also even more that

27
00:01:19.696 --> 00:01:21.782
deal with loading
unstructured data from

28
00:01:21.782 --> 00:01:24.911
the proprietary data
sources that you or your company

29
00:01:24.911 --> 00:01:26.650
might have, like Figma, Notion.


30
00:01:28.378 --> 00:01:31.548
Document loaders can also be
used to load structured data.

31
00:01:31.548 --> 00:01:34.718
Data that's in a tabular format
and may just have

32
00:01:34.718 --> 00:01:38.522
some text data in one of those cells
or rows that you

33
00:01:38.522 --> 00:01:41.058
still want to do
question answering or semantic

34
00:01:41.058 --> 00:01:43.278
search over. And
so the sources here

35
00:01:43.278 --> 00:01:45.180
include things like Airbyte, Stripe,
Airtable.

36
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.682
Alright, now let's
jump into the fun stuff, actually

37
00:01:49.682 --> 00:01:53.132
using document loaders. First,
we're going to load some environment variables

38
00:01:53.132 --> 00:01:55.641
that we need, like the
OpenAI API key.

39
00:01:57.101 --> 00:02:02.581
The first type of documents that we're
going to be working with are PDFs. So

40
00:02:02.581 --> 00:02:06.600
let's import the relevant document loader
from Langchain. We're going to

41
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.062
use the PyPDF loader.

42
00:02:10.924 --> 00:02:13.538
We've loaded a bunch of
PDFs into the documents

43
00:02:13.538 --> 00:02:15.862
folder in the workspace, and
so let's choose

44
00:02:15.862 --> 00:02:17.895
one and put that in the loader.

45
00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.322
Now let's load the documents by just
calling the load method.

46
00:02:31.541 --> 00:02:34.316
Let's
take a look at what exactly we've loaded.

47
00:02:34.316 --> 00:02:37.399
So this, by default, will load
a list of documents.

48
00:02:42.541 --> 00:02:45.596
In this case, there are 22 different
pages in this PDF.

49
00:02:45.596 --> 00:02:47.539
Each one is its own unique document.

50
00:02:48.223 --> 00:02:50.480
Let's take a look at the first one
and see what it consists of.

51
00:02:51.922 --> 00:02:54.326
The first thing
the document consists of is some

52
00:02:54.326 --> 00:02:56.730
page content,
which is the content of the page.

53
00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.935
This can be a bit long, so let's
just print out the

54
00:02:59.935 --> 00:03:01.004
first few hundred characters.

55
00:03:14.724 --> 00:03:18.729
The other piece of information that's
really important is the metadata associated

56
00:03:18.729 --> 00:03:21.400
with each document. This
can be accessed with

57
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:22.401
the metadata element.

58
00:03:26.718 --> 00:03:29.404
You can see here that there's
two different pieces. One

59
00:03:29.404 --> 00:03:31.821
is the source information. This
is the PDF, the

60
00:03:31.821 --> 00:03:34.506
name of the file that
we loaded it from. The

61
00:03:34.506 --> 00:03:37.192
other is the page field. This
corresponds to the page

62
00:03:37.192 --> 00:03:39.340
of the PDF that it was loaded from.


63
00:03:40.838 --> 00:03:42.421
The next type of
document loader that we're

64
00:03:42.421 --> 00:03:44.400
going to look at is
one that loads from YouTube.

65
00:03:44.777 --> 00:03:47.336
There's
a lot of fun content on YouTube, and

66
00:03:47.336 --> 00:03:50.747
so a lot of people use this document
loader to be able

67
00:03:50.747 --> 00:03:53.021
to ask questions of
their favorite videos or

68
00:03:53.021 --> 00:03:54.442
lectures or anything like that.

69
00:03:57.839 --> 00:04:00.912
We're going to import a
few different things here. The

70
00:04:00.912 --> 00:04:03.370
key parts are the
YouTube audio loader, which

71
00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:06.136
loads an audio file
from a YouTube video. The

72
00:04:06.136 --> 00:04:08.901
other key part is
the OpenAI Whisper parser. This

73
00:04:08.901 --> 00:04:11.974
will use OpenAI's Whisper
model, a speech-to-text model, to convert

74
00:04:11.974 --> 00:04:15.047
the YouTube audio into a text
format that we can

75
00:04:15.047 --> 00:04:15.662
work with.

76
00:04:18.241 --> 00:04:22.654
We can now specify a URL,
specify a directory in which

77
00:04:22.654 --> 00:04:27.067
to save the audio files,
and then create the generic loader

78
00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:30.678
as a combination of this YouTube
audio loader combined

79
00:04:30.678 --> 00:04:35.492
with the OpenAI Whisper parser.
And then we can call "loader.load" to

80
00:04:35.492 --> 00:04:38.300
load the documents corresponding
to this YouTube.

81
00:04:39.498 --> 00:04:43.993
This may take a few minutes, so we're
going to speed this up and post. Now

82
00:04:43.993 --> 00:04:47.364
that it's finished loading, we can
take a look at the page

83
00:04:47.364 --> 00:04:50.735
content of what we've loaded. And
this is the first part of

84
00:04:50.735 --> 00:04:53.263
the transcript from the
YouTube video. This is a

85
00:04:53.263 --> 00:04:56.915
good time to pause, go choose your
favorite YouTube video, and see if

86
00:04:56.915 --> 00:04:58.320
this transcription works for
you.

87
00:04:59.482 --> 00:05:02.504
The next set of documents that we're
going to go over how to

88
00:05:02.504 --> 00:05:03.899
load are URLs from the Internet.

89
00:05:05.158 --> 00:05:08.062
There's a lot of really awesome educational
content on the Internet, and

90
00:05:08.062 --> 00:05:10.723
wouldn't it be cool if you could
just chat with it?

91
00:05:10.723 --> 00:05:14.339
We're going to enable that
by importing the web-based loader

92
00:05:14.339 --> 00:05:17.956
from LangChain. Then we
can choose any URL, our favorite

93
00:05:17.956 --> 00:05:21.210
URL. Here, we're going to
choose a markdown file

94
00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:24.103
from this GitHub page
and create a loader

95
00:05:24.103 --> 00:05:28.443
for it. And then next,
we can call loader.load, and then we

96
00:05:28.443 --> 00:05:33.506
can take a look at the content
of the page. Here, you'll notice there's

97
00:05:33.506 --> 00:05:37.484
a lot of white space,
followed by some initial text, and

98
00:05:37.484 --> 00:05:41.100
then some more text. This
is a good example of

99
00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:46.163
why you actually need to do some post-processing
on the information to get it

100
00:05:46.163 --> 00:05:47.609
into a workable
format.

101
00:05:47.609 --> 00:05:47.609


102
00:05:47.609 --> 00:05:51.220
Finally, we'll
cover how to load data from Notion. Notion

103
00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:54.109
is a really popular
store of both personal

104
00:05:54.109 --> 00:05:56.997
and company data, and
a lot of people

105
00:05:56.997 --> 00:06:00.247
have created chatbots talking
to their Notion databases. In

106
00:06:00.247 --> 00:06:03.858
your notebook, you'll see
instructions on how to export data

107
00:06:03.858 --> 00:06:07.108
from your Notion
database into a format through which

108
00:06:07.108 --> 00:06:09.274
we can load it into LangChain.


109
00:06:09.374 --> 00:06:12.664
Once we have it in that format,
we can use the

110
00:06:12.664 --> 00:06:15.056
Notion directory loader to
load that data and

111
00:06:15.056 --> 00:06:19.840
get documents that we can work with.
If we take a look at the content here,

112
00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:22.532
we can see that it's
in markdown format, and

113
00:06:22.532 --> 00:06:25.821
this Notion document is from Blendle's
Employee Handbook. I'm sure a

114
00:06:25.821 --> 00:06:28.214
lot of people listening
have used Notion and

115
00:06:28.214 --> 00:06:30.606
have some Notion databases
that they would like

116
00:06:30.606 --> 00:06:34.194
to chat with, and so this
is a great opportunity to go

117
00:06:34.194 --> 00:06:37.185
export that data, bring it in
here, and start working

118
00:06:37.185 --> 00:06:38.680
with it in this
format.

119
00:06:39.978 --> 00:06:43.590
That's it for document loading. Here,
we've covered how to load

120
00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:47.532
data from a variety of sources
and get it into a standardized

121
00:06:47.532 --> 00:06:49.174
document interface. However, these
documents

122
00:06:49.174 --> 00:06:53.443
are still rather large, and so
in the next section, we're going to

123
00:06:53.443 --> 00:06:57.713
go over how to split them up into
smaller chunks. This is relevant

124
00:06:57.713 --> 00:07:00.340
and important because when
you're doing this retrieval

125
00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:02.311
augmented generation, you
need to retrieve

126
00:07:02.311 --> 00:07:04.938
only the pieces of
content that are most

127
00:07:04.938 --> 00:07:09.208
relevant, and so you don't
want to select the whole documents that we've

128
00:07:09.208 --> 00:07:12.821
loaded here, but rather only
the paragraph or few sentences that

129
00:07:12.821 --> 00:07:15.448
are most topical to what you're
talking about.

130
00:07:15.448 --> 00:07:15.448


131
00:07:16.797 --> 00:07:19.332
This is also an
even better opportunity to

132
00:07:19.332 --> 00:07:21.867
think about what sources
of data we don't

133
00:07:21.867 --> 00:07:25.670
currently have loaders for, but
you might still want to explore. Who

134
00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:28.839
knows? Maybe you can even make
a PR to LangChain.
